Exercise part 1:
=============================
=      File description     =
=============================
BrickerGameManager.java - implements the BrickGameManager class which contains
 the main method and extends GameManager class.
bricked_strategies package - contains the class CollisionStrategy.
CollisionStrategy.java - implements the ColissionStrategy class.
gameobjects package - contains the files: Ball, Brick, Paddle,
 GraphicaLifeCounter and NumericalLifeCounter. each one of them 
 implements the relevant class and extends GameObject class.

=============================
=          Design           =
=============================
I implemented the BrickerGameManager class so that it contains the initializer method 
Which is responsible for creating all the gameobjects needed for the game. It also
contains update method which run every frame update (each gameobject class has a Constructor 
and update method).
bricked_strategies package contains CollisionStrategy class which is responsible for
Cases when ball collides with brick.
I used this design following the design of DanoGameLab.
I designed BrickerGameManager in this way so that I will not be forced to call all the 
Constructors of all classes in the main, so that there will be only one class which is 
Responsible for initializing all objects in the game so that only this class will be 
called in the main.
Add to that, the update method in BrickerGameManager will be responsible for updating all 
what is on screen.
I added the CollisionStrategy class so that it will be responsible of removing bricks when 
A ball hits them, in order to not do this in Brick class which is not responsible for
collisions.

Exercise part 2:
I wrote CollisionStrategy interface. I also wrote a RemoverBrickStrategyDecorator, this decorator
is an abstract class. This abstract class implements the CollisionStrategy interface , and then,
all the Strategies we have in the game (except the RemoveBrickStrategy) extends the
RemoverBrickStrategyDecorator.
The RemoveBrickStrategy which is responsible for removing a brick when it collides only
implements the CollisionStrategy interface. At the start of the game, every brick has a
specific Strategy,this strategy is taken randomly by BrickStrategyFactory, which gives
 a random Strategy.
Each of these Strategies is given as a parameter RemoveBrickStrategy object which is use by
the RemoverBrickStrategyDecorator decorator to remove The brick, 
in addition to what the Strategy do other than removing the brick.
Each strategy adds an appropriate gameObject.
I used this implementation following the API we have in the exercise and DanoGameLab.

Algorithms:

1)alotStudyTime:
 i)n = tasks.length, m = timeSlots.length.
   first, i sorted the tasks and timeSlots in an ascending order. i moved in a while loop with one
   index on each array. If timeSlots[i] >= tasks[j], I add 1 to the number of tasks we can make.
   So if the arrays length is 1, so it is clear that the method will work. Assume that the
   method will Work on task length of n-1, so we compare all the time slots we didn't check them, 
   If one j index timeSlots[j] >= tasks[n], we add 1. And then we exit the loop (increase i by 1).
   Else, we increase index j by one and, we don't change the number of tasks, and exit the loop.
 ii)Time complexity: we have a loop that ends when one of the indexes reaches n = tasks.length,
    or m = timeSlots.length. In each loop we increase at least one of the indexes by 1.
    so we are looping on a loop of length of min(n , m). I sorted the two arrays, the sorting 
    I used works in O(nlog(n)) which is larger. So the time complexity is O(nlog(n) + mlog(m)).
   
2)minLeap:
 ii)Time Complexity:
    The longest thing we do is a loop of length n = leapNum.length.
    all other operations are O(1) so we Get O(n).

3)bucketWalk:
 i)the base case is when there is 0,1 or 2 liters of water in well.
   For 0,1 we return 1. For 2 we return 2.I use here dynamic programming, i defined an array sums.
   Define sums[n] to be the number of ways to fill trough with n liters. I saw that sums[n] =
   sums[n-1] + sums[n-2]. So i wrote a loop, each time doing sums[j] = sums[j-1] + sums[j-2].
   if we assumed that the algorithm work for n-1 and n-2, so in the last Iteration in the loop
   we will have sums[n] = sums[n-1] + sums[n-2], and so we return sums[n].
 ii)Time complexity: we have a loop of length n, so the time is O(n).(all other operations are O(1).

4)
 i)numTrees: I did something similar to bucketWalk, for every 1 <= n <= 19,
 numOfTrees[n] = numOfTrees[0] * numOfTrees[n-1] + numOfTrees[1] * numOfTrees[n-1]
 + ... + numberOfTrees[n-1] * numberOfTrees[0].
 So we did a loop that matches this.
 ii)Time compexity: we have a loop inside a loop, so O(n^2).
